<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How GuideHollow researches primary documentation, builds independently verifiable browser tools, distinguishes illustrations from observed runs, and handles corrections." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Editorial Policy',url:'/editorial-policy/'}]}>
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow mint">/ editorial_policy</p><h1>Clarity starts<br />with honest limits.</h1><p>Published October 23, 2026. Updated November 3, 2026. Our standards for original, reproducible, source-verified AI guides and browser utilities.</p></header><article class="prose info-content"><h2>Our purpose</h2><p>GuideHollow publishes original, verifiable workflows for people using AI tools in everyday writing, spreadsheets, research, and file management. Every guide starts from a concrete failure mode—such as an invented citation, a truncated spreadsheet range, or a leaked API credential—and pairs clear prose with downloadable inputs or interactive browser checks that readers can test themselves.</p><p>We maintain a focused, high-signal library rather than generating high-volume summaries. Whenever a reader follows one of our guides, they should be able to inspect the exact source notes, run the calculation or checklist locally without creating an account, and tell immediately what a check proves versus what still requires human judgment.</p><h2>Primary source verification</h2><p>Every technical claim about product features, privacy settings, file upload limits, sharing permissions, or spreadsheet formulas is checked against primary documentation—such as official Help Center specifications, W3C accessibility standards, or official vendor documentation—cited directly at the bottom of each guide.</p><p>We avoid affiliate hype, sponsored tool rankings, fabricated model benchmarks, or unverified speculation. Because product plans, regional privacy settings, and interface behavior change over time, every article lists the exact date its external documentation was reviewed alongside any structural updates.</p><h2>Authorship and editorial verification</h2><p>Guides are authored, verified, and maintained under the <strong>GuideHollow Editorial</strong> standard by the site maintainer using primary official documentation, deterministic code fixtures, and reproducible browser-based verification suites. All source files, automated verification scripts, and correction history are published transparently in our public repository so readers and reviewers can trace how every published rule and example was checked.</p><p>When an example dataset is designed specifically for teaching—such as our sample expense CSV or claim-boundary notes—we explicitly label it as a constructed teaching fixture so readers never confuse a controlled classroom exercise with customer survey data or third-party telemetry.</p><h2>Three-tier evidence framework</h2><p>To help readers apply strict verification in their own work, every guide distinguishes three clear classes of published evidence:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence type</th><th>What it contains</th><th>Example on GuideHollow</th><th>What it establishes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Editorial workflow pair</td><td>Controlled before-and-after inputs and boundary-preserving drafts</td><td><a href="/guides/how-to-draft-emails-with-ai/">Email revision guide</a></td><td>How strict constraints keep dates, attendees, and uncertainties intact</td></tr><tr><td>Reproducible interactive tool</td><td>Downloadable CSV/TSV/PDF/XLSX inputs plus deterministic browser code</td><td><a href="/tools/weighted-price-checker/">Weighted Average Audit</a></td><td>Exact numerical or rule-based outcomes anyone can independently repeat</td></tr><tr><td>Primary source audit</td><td>Side-by-side comparison against current official documentation</td><td><a href="/ai-tools/how-to-use-chatgpt-search/">ChatGPT Search source guide</a></td><td>Which product capability matches published documentation on the review date</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><strong>Editorial example:</strong> an answer constructed to demonstrate a method. The <a href="/guides/how-to-draft-emails-with-ai/">email revision pair</a> shows what should be preserved; it is not a captured model run.</li><li><strong>Observed run:</strong> an output recorded from a stated interaction. The <a href="/ai-tools/getting-started-with-chatgpt/">two-turn beginner exercise</a> includes its request, response, follow-up, and run limits. One successful run is not a reliability estimate.</li><li><strong>Source check:</strong> a claim compared with an original reference. The <a href="/guides/how-to-verify-ai-generated-information/">claim ledger</a> separates supported, contradicted, and unresolved claims. A source check does not prove an account action happened.</li></ul><p>When an observed answer fails a check, the recorded output and editorial correction stay clearly separated. For example, our <a href="/guides/how-to-summarize-long-documents-with-ai/">document summary review</a> highlights an unsupported financial overstatement directly rather than silently editing the original example.</p><h2>Reproducing calculations and automated test coverage</h2><p>For spreadsheet, timestamp, JSON, or accessibility guides, GuideHollow ships original datasets alongside browser-side calculators and automated regression suites. Our <a href="/guides/verify-ai-weighted-average-price-spreadsheet/">weighted-price walkthrough</a> provides the original <a href="/examples/weighted-price-sample.csv">eight-row CSV fixture</a>, an <a href="/tools/weighted-price-checker/">independent in-browser calculator</a>, and deliberate error-injection checks for blank cells and channel mismatches. Readers can repeat every sum in Excel, LibreOffice Calc, or our browser workbench without trusting opaque model arithmetic.</p><p>For prompt-evaluation tutorials, our <a href="/examples/prompt-evaluation-routing.csv">eight-case routing dataset</a> pairs defined inputs with expected review labels, and our <a href="/prompts/test-ai-prompts-with-evaluation-dataset/">evaluation guide</a> shows how to measure accuracy versus formatting defects step by step.</p><h2>Firsthand tool audits and downloadable fixtures</h2><p><strong>Directly observed browser use:</strong> on November 1, 2026, we operated the public GuideHollow Weighted Average Audit Workbench in an interactive Chromium session, verified its row-level sums and blank-price error rejection, and published the complete <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">dated results ledger</a> and <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/docs/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.md">reproduction procedure</a>. When a parallel quiz run revealed a pre-submit input edge case, we disclosed the finding transparently and updated the browser validation guard before shipping further guides.</p><p>Our <a href="/evidence/">Evidence Library</a> organizes source notes, answer keys, PDF OCR fixtures, Unicode cleanup files, and CSV checks under clear licensing and explicit scope limits. For instance, the <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">source notes</a> and <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-audit.csv">22-case answer key</a> are stored separately so readers can classify statements independently before inspecting our verified key via our <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/tests/claim-boundary-audit.test.mjs">automated verification test</a>.</p><p>Our <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/docs/editorial-fact-check-2026-11-01.md">31-article audit register</a> records source checks across launch guides and distinguishes archived software captures from current automated test coverage. Quality maintenance includes updating affected articles whenever primary product docs change or a reader submits a documented correction.</p><h2>Operational transparency and public readiness checks</h2><p>On November 2, 2026, our <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/actions/runs/41540809663">GitHub Pages deployment log</a> recorded an automated HTTP check across 16 key public routes. One path was deliberately nonexistent and correctly returned 404. Publishing the exact <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/scripts/verify-public-readiness.mjs">checker script</a> ensures availability claims remain tied to observable deployment checks rather than vague assertions, though availability checks alone do not represent a sitewide user study or Google AdSense review decision. Our <a href="/guides/what-is-generative-ai/">introductory guide</a> teaches beginners to apply the same distinction between observed logs and general claims.</p><h2>Publication dates, updates, and reader corrections</h2><p>Publication dates record when a guide first passed editorial review and entered the public catalog. Updated dates change only when factual content, source documentation, worked exercises, or tool behavior materially improves; typographical fixes do not bump revision timestamps.</p><p>Readers can report an outdated instruction, broken external URL, or arithmetic question at any time through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> with the exact article URL and supporting reference. Verified corrections update both the published guide and the public commit log.</p><h2>Editorial independence and monetization boundaries</h2><p>GuideHollow maintains strict editorial independence: guide rankings, examples, and tool checks are never paid placements or affiliate promotions. If display advertising via AdSense is later activated after site review and consent setup, advertising containers remain visually labeled and structurally separated from article workflows and tool controls.</p><h2>Educational scope and professional boundaries</h2><p>Our guides, prompts, and utilities are educational and productivity resources. They do not substitute for licensed legal, medical, accounting, tax, or cybersecurity incident advice. For high-stakes workplace or regulated decisions, verify every result against authoritative primary sources and qualified human reviewers.</p></article>
